          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nLong-term use is something the retrieved guidance treats as needing periodic review. It does not say that six years of use is harmful. The gastroenterology guideline (GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it) says that where long-term acid suppression is genuinely needed, for example for erosive esophagitis, Barrett's esophagus, or symptoms that return when the medicine is stopped, it is continued \"at the lowest effective intensity with periodic review, rather than renewed indefinitely without reassessment.\" The retrieved passages did not cover the risks or side effects of long-term use, so I can't tell you what those are from these sources. Whether your own treatment still fits your situation is a question for the clinician who prescribes it. Please don't change or stop it on your own.\n\nOn review, the same guideline supports asking whether the original reason for the medicine still applies. A useful question for your next appointment is: \"Do I still need this, and at what dose, and when will we review it?\"\n\nOn coding, the ICD-10-CM coding summary (Coding gastroesophageal reflux disease: K21) says gastroesophageal reflux disease is category K21 in Chapter 11, the digestive system chapter. The main distinction is whether esophagitis is present, and that must be documented from an examination finding, not inferred from symptom severity, the treatment prescribed, or how long the condition has lasted. Most records use the \"without esophagitis\" code because the finding usually isn't recorded. The same summary says long-term drug therapy status is coded from a separate chapter, but the passages did not name the specific code, so I can't give it. Coding is done by clinicians and coders from medical records, so this is background rather than something to apply to yourself.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
